# Nova: Infinix X6525 (ums9230) GKI 2.0 build overrides.
#
# Merge order: gki_defconfig -> sprd_gki_sharkl6.fragment -> this fragment.
#
# !! COMMENT HYGIENE FOR THIS FILE !!
# merge_config.sh builds its directive list from two anchored patterns:
#     ^CONFIG_<SYM>=<value>
#     ^# CONFIG_<SYM> is not set$      (whole line, nothing else)
# A comment that merely mentions a symbol together with a value, or that is
# exactly "# CONFIG_X is not set", gets ingested as a real directive.  That
# does not corrupt .config (kconfig still sees only the last real assignment,
# and out-of-order lines can bite), but it does make the merge log print the
# comment back as the "New value", which is miserable to debug from.  So:
# refer to symbols below WITHOUT a value, and never write a bare
# "# CONFIG_... is not set" line inside a comment.

# --- LTO -------------------------------------------------------------------
# gki_defconfig turns on full LTO, whose single-process `LTO vmlinux.o` link
# peaks above the ~14 GiB a ubuntu-22.04 runner has and gets OOM-killed -- the
# job dies with exit 143 immediately after printing "LTO vmlinux.o".  ThinLTO
# is the same whole-program optimisation model (cross-module inlining) but it
# parallelises the backend, so peak RSS drops by an order of magnitude.  It
# also keeps CFI_CLANG valid, which matters because CFI_CLANG depends on
# LTO_CLANG, so plain "switch LTO off" is not an available option here.
CONFIG_LTO_CLANG_FULL=n
CONFIG_LTO_CLANG_THIN=y

# --- Display (hard requirement) -------------------------------------------
# sprd_gki_sharkl6.fragment disables the Unisoc display platform outright,
# which leaves the entire display stack out of the build -- a guaranteed black
# screen.  The running 5.4 device loads BOTH sprd_drm.ko and sprd_gsp.ko, and
# the X6525 device tree's display nodes bind only to drivers that live inside
# sprd-drm.ko:
#
#   soc/ap-ahb/dsi@31100000  qogirl6-dsi-host   -> sprd_disp/sprd_dsi.c
#   dphy                     qogirl6-dsi-phy    -> sprd_disp/sprd_dphy.c
#   .../panel                generic-mipi-panel -> sprd_disp/sprd_dsi_panel.c
#                             (mipi_dsi_driver table)
#
# UNISOC_DISP is tristate and its Kconfig help names sprd_drm as the resulting
# module, so keep it modular to match the device's own module set and load
# order instead of baking ~1 MB of display code into the GKI Image.  sprd-drm
# pulls in disp_trusty.o (gated on TRUSTY_VIRTIO_IPC), so the trusty modules
# must be insmod'd BEFORE sprd-drm.ko.
CONFIG_UNISOC_DISP=m
CONFIG_DRM_SPRD_DPU0=y
CONFIG_DRM_SPRD_DSI=y
CONFIG_UNISOC_GSP=m

# --- WiFi / WCN (integrated Marlin) ---------------------------------------
# sprd_gki_sharkl6.fragment sets SC23XX (the parent) but leaves WCN_INTEG off,
# so sprdwcn builds with no transport selected and WiFi never enumerates.
# The X6525 uses the INTEGRATED Marlin WCN, not a PCIe one:
#   * our live device tree has cpwcn-btwf compatible = "unisoc,integrate_marlin"
#     and sipc-virt/core@3/wcn_bt compatible = "sprd,wcn_internal_chip";
#   * the tree's own arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums9230/wcn.dtsi uses those exact
#     same two compatibles, and drivers/unisoc_platform/sprdwcn/sprd_wcn.c
#     binds integrate_marlin to g_integ_wcn_data;
#   * the running 5.4 device loads sprd_wlan_combo, sprdbt_tty and wcn_bsp and
#     has WCN_INTEG built in.
# PCIE_SPRD stays off, matching the device (the WCN is on-chip, not over PCIe).
# SC2355 is deliberately left off: the device has it off too, and the driver
# probes the Marlin revision at runtime.
CONFIG_WCN_INTEG=y

# --- Ramoops / pstore (crash persistence) ----------------------------------
# Keep the kernel's oops/panic records in RAM across reboot.  This is our ONLY
# diagnostic channel: the board has no dtb/dtbo partition, no FDT inside
# boot.img and no serial console, so when a 5.15 boot attempt goes wrong the
# pstore record is the single artefact that tells us why.  Every boot attempt
# should leave a trail.
#
# Symbol-name traps here, all of which would silently build nothing:
#   * Upstream renamed PSTORE_RAMOOPS -> PSTORE_RAM back in 5.10.  The *module*
#     is still called ramoops.ko (see the PSTORE_RAM help text), but the
#     Kconfig symbol is PSTORE_RAM.  Writing the old name produces a phantom
#     line in .config and no ramoops at all.
#   * The compression choice is PSTORE_DEFLATE_COMPRESS, not PSTORE_DEFLATE,
#     and the *default* is a separate bool in a choice group.
# gki_defconfig already turns on PSTORE, PSTORE_CONSOLE, PSTORE_PMSG and
# PSTORE_RAM; pinning them here means a future defconfig bump cannot quietly
# drop the crash log, and the compress options keep 256 KiB of ramoops holding
# a useful amount of text.
#
# The matching device-tree node is reserved-memory/ramoops@fff80000 -- 256 KiB
# with 32 KiB each for record/console/pmsg.  Note it lives in the *overlay*
# dtsi, and we deliberately do NOT flash our own DTB: the stock X6525 DTB
# already carries this node, which is why no DT change is needed here.
CONFIG_PSTORE=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_PMSG=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_COMPRESS=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_DEFLATE_COMPRESS=y
CONFIG_PSTORE_DEFLATE_COMPRESS_DEFAULT=y

# --- Panic / hang capture: match the running 5.4 device --------------------
# The device's own /proc/config.gz has SPRD_SYSDUMP, SPRD_MINI_SYSDUMP and
# SPRD_NATIVE_HANG_MONITOR all built IN, while the vendor 5.15 fragment ships
# the first and third as modules.  That difference matters: these register
# panic and hard-hang notifiers, so if the module has not been insmod'd by the
# time things go wrong, the handler is not resident and no dump gets written.
# A module is strictly worse here than the configuration the vendor itself
# ships and boots on this SoC, so promote them to built-in.
#
# Deliberate exception to "keep the GKI Image lean": these are correctness and
# diagnostic paths for a board we cannot serial-console, not a performance
# change, and they are what the vendor validated on ums9230.  SPRD_SYSDUMP also
# gates on !KEXEC_CORE, which the merged config leaves off.
CONFIG_SPRD_SYSDUMP=y
CONFIG_SPRD_NATIVE_HANG_MONITOR=y

# --- Deliberately NOT enabled (flagged for review) -------------------------
# UNISOC_MINIDUMP_FTRACE copies the ftrace ring buffer into the sysdump
# minidump on oops -- genuinely useful for a bring-up port.  It is off in the
# vendor fragment, and gki_defconfig enables no tracing at all (no
# CONFIG_TRACING / CONFIG_FTRACE), so the ring buffer this code drains would
# never be populated.  Doing it properly means adding ftrace to the GKI Image
# plus 8 KiB * UNISOC_MINIDUMP_FTRACE_SIZE_KB (2 MiB at the default 256) of
# per-CPU buffer -- too much for a first boot we want to stay minimal.
# Recipe when it is wanted:
#   CONFIG_TRACING=y
#   CONFIG_FTRACE=y
#   CONFIG_UNISOC_MINIDUMP_FTRACE=y
#   CONFIG_UNISOC_MINIDUMP_FTRACE_SIZE_KB=256
#
# UNISOC_CACHEDUMP is blocked the same way: it depends on SPRD_DEBUG, which the
# vendor fragment turns off.  cachedump hands debug info to u-boot to write to
# eMMC after a sysdump, so it is only worth having once we know the u-boot
# half of that path runs -- and flipping the broad SPRD_DEBUG switch to find
# out is not a trade worth making before the phone boots.

# --- modules the X6525's own modules.load asks for, that nothing enabled ----
#
# Both of these were found by ci/check-module-coverage.sh diffing our build
# against the device's /vendor/lib/modules/modules.load, and both were absent
# from the generated config entirely -- not set to "n", simply never defined, so
# no .ko was produced and nothing in the tree complained.
#
# CONFIG_UNISOC_GPU_COOLING_DEVICE -> sprd_gpu_cooling.ko
#   The device's own 5.4 config has CONFIG_SPRD_GPU_COOLING_DEVICE=m, i.e. this
#   is the same option under its post-rename name (SPRD_ -> UNISOC_).  Built
#   from drivers/unisoc_platform/thermal/Makefile.  Without it the GPU has no
#   thermal throttling path, which on this SoC is how the Mali driver is kept
#   from cooking the phone.
#
# CONFIG_ION_SPRD -> sprd-ion.ko
#   drivers/staging/android/ion/sprd/Makefile.  The device loads sprd-ion.ko
#   from modules.load, and the out-of-tree camera units hard-depend on it:
#   common/camera/core/Makefile and common/camera/cam_sys/Makefile both do
#     KBUILD_EXTRA_SYMBOLS += $(BSP_MODULES_OUT)/sprd-ion/Module.symvers
#   so without it those units cannot resolve ion symbols even once they compile.
#   Note the device's 5.4 config says "# CONFIG_ION_SPRD is not set", yet it
#   ships and loads sprd-ion.ko -- in 5.4 the driver hung off a different
#   symbol.  ION_SPRD is the 5.15 name, and the load list plus the camera
#   dependency are the authority here, not the old config value.
CONFIG_UNISOC_GPU_COOLING_DEVICE=m

# ION.  The device's own 5.4 config is the reference for the values:
#   CONFIG_ION=y
#   CONFIG_ION_SYSTEM_HEAP=y
#   CONFIG_ION_CMA_HEAP=m      -> ion_cma_heap.ko, which modules.load names
# and ION_SPRD is the 5.15 name for sprd-ion.ko.
#
# CONFIG_ION_SPRD depends on ION, so ION has to be enabled first -- setting
# ION_SPRD alone silently produces nothing, which is exactly what happened on
# the first attempt (the symbol was absent from the generated config and no
# error was reported anywhere).
#
# The device's 5.4 config says "# CONFIG_ION_SPRD is not set" yet it ships and
# loads sprd-ion.ko, so 5.4 hung that driver off some other switch.  The load
# list and the camera units' KBUILD_EXTRA_SYMBOLS reference are the authority
# for 5.15, not the old config value.
CONFIG_ION=y
CONFIG_ION_SYSTEM_HEAP=y
CONFIG_ION_CMA_HEAP=m
CONFIG_ION_SPRD=m

# CONFIG_UNISOC_DMABUF=m -> sprd-dmabuf.ko
#   Another module.load entry with no config behind it, found the same way as
#   the ION group.  drivers/unisoc_platform/dmabufheap/Makefile.  It is a
#   provider, not a leaf: common/camera/{core,cam_sys,cpp} all do
#     KBUILD_EXTRA_SYMBOLS += $(BSP_MODULES_OUT)/sprd-dmabuf/Module.symvers
#   so until this exists the camera stack cannot resolve its symbols.
CONFIG_UNISOC_DMABUF=m
